---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

// A named author with a real biography is an expertise signal, and an honest
// one is worth more than an inflated one. The claim made here is COMPUTATIONAL,
// not advisory — that is the only claim this project can defend, and stating it
// plainly is a differentiator rather than an admission.

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    url: `${SITE.url}/about`,
    mainEntity: {
      '@type': 'Person',
      name: SITE.author.name,
      url: `${SITE.url}/about`,
      sameAs: [SITE.author.github],
      knowsAbout: ['Numerical accuracy', 'Amortisation', 'Web performance'],
    },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE.url}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'About', item: `${SITE.url}/about` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About QuickOper and who writes it"
  description="QuickOper is built by Vikash Singh, a software engineer. We are not financial advisers — we are engineers who do the arithmetic correctly and publish our tests. Here is exactly what that means."
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <article class="text-ink-soft space-y-4">
    <h1 class="text-ink text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">About QuickOper</h1>

    <p class="text-lg">
      QuickOper makes money calculators that show their working, prove their arithmetic,
      and never send anything you type to a server.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-ink pt-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Who writes this</h2>

    <p>
      QuickOper is built and maintained by{' '}
      <strong class="text-ink">{SITE.author.name}</strong>, a software engineer.
      Everything on the site — the calculations, the tests, the writing — is his work, and
      the source is public at{' '}
      <a class="text-brand underline" href={SITE.repo} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="border-line bg-sunken rounded-panel border p-4">
      <p class="text-ink font-semibold">
        We are not financial advisers, and this is not financial advice.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Nobody here holds a financial qualification. That is stated at the top rather than
        buried in a footer, because it changes what you should and should not use this
        site for.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-ink pt-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
      What we are actually claiming to be good at
    </h2>

    <p>
      There is a real difference between <em>advice</em> and <em>arithmetic</em>, and
      QuickOper only does the second.
    </p>

    <p>
      Telling you whether to clear your credit card before topping up your pension is
      advice. It depends on your job security, your tax position, your family, your
      temperament and your plans, and it needs a qualified person who knows all of that.
      We will never do it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Working out that adding $200 a month to a particular balance removes four years and
      $3,140 of interest, and showing you every month of the schedule that gets there —
      that is arithmetic. It is verifiable, it is checkable against published figures, and
      it is something a careful engineer can get exactly right.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the expertise being claimed here is <strong class="text-ink"
        >computational</strong
      >, not advisory: that the numbers are correct, the method is disclosed, and the
      tests are public. <a class="text-brand underline" href="/methodology"
        >How we verify our numbers</a
      >{' '}
      sets out precisely how that is checked, including a worked example you can repeat.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-ink pt-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
      Why the site exists
    </h2>

    <p>
      Most free calculators give you a single number and no way to check it. You cannot
      see the formula, you cannot see the months in between, you cannot tell whether the
      assumptions match your situation, and you certainly cannot verify the result. If the
      answer is wrong you have no way of knowing.
    </p>

    <p>
      Meanwhile the tools that do show detail usually want your email address first, and
      send your figures to a server on the way.
    </p>

    <p>QuickOper is the opposite of both. Every tool gives you:</p>

    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
      <li>the formula, and the intermediate values it produced;</li>
      <li>every period of the schedule, not just the total;</li>
      <li>the assumptions, and where they stop matching reality;</li>
      <li>the result as a spreadsheet or a printable document you can keep;</li>
      <li>no sign-up, no email, no tracking of what you typed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-ink pt-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
      Nothing you type leaves your device
    </h2>

    <p>
      This is a structural fact, not a promise we are asking you to take on faith.
      QuickOper is a static site with no backend and no database. The calculators are
      JavaScript running in your browser, on your machine. There is no server that could
      receive your balances, because there is no server at all.
    </p>

    <p>
      Shareable links carry the numbers so a scenario can be reopened — but never the
      names you give your debts. See <a class="text-brand underline" href="/privacy"
        >privacy</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-ink pt-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Corrections</h2>

    <p>
      If a figure here disagrees with your lender or an official worked example, that is
      worth knowing and we want to hear about it. Corrections are made openly, with the
      change and its date recorded on the affected page. Email{' '}
      <a class="text-brand underline" href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> or open
      an issue on <a class="text-brand underline" href={SITE.repo} rel="noopener"
        >GitHub</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-ink pt-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
      How this is paid for
    </h2>

    <p>
      Nothing on QuickOper is behind a payment or a sign-up. In future the site may carry
      advertising, or links to products where we would be paid a commission. When that
      happens it will be labelled clearly on the page it appears on, and it will never
      change which answer a calculator gives you — the arithmetic does not know or care
      what is being advertised beside it.
    </p>

    <p class="text-ink-mute pt-4 text-sm">Last updated {SITE.legalLastUpdated}.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
